Subject: [PATCH 68/68] libopenbios: an ELF gate in front of the segment copy -- refuse a malformed, mis-classed or self-overlapping image BY NAME before `load` moves a byte; init-program zeroes state-valid and names an unrecognised image; amd64 loads its own class

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

THE IRREVERSIBLE STEP OF A `load` IS NOT `go`. forth/debugging/client.fs's $load
calls init-program the moment the device's load method returns, and
elf_init_program() copies every PT_LOAD to its p_vaddr before it sets up any
program state. Nothing stood in front of that copy: no check of a segment
against the bytes actually loaded, none of the destination against the firmware
doing the copying, and none of the image's class beyond is_elf()'s silent no.
Measured 2026-09-05, on all four of this lab's doors, before this patch:

  x86    `load` of obj-x86/openbios-builtin.elf -- the firmware's own coreboot
         payload, PT_LOAD at 0x100000, which is where the running firmware's
         code lives in its own (segment-relocated) address space -- never
         returned. The copy went over the code performing it.
  ppc    `load` of obj-ppc/openbios-qemu.elf (PT_LOAD at 0xfff00000, the ROM
         the firmware runs from) printed "Ignoring failed claim for va
         fff00000 memsz b5730!" and hung; the pty driver's echo gate timed out.
  amd64  `load` of a 32-bit i386 client program (the sibling lab's hello-x86)
         was ACCEPTED: state-valid -1, and `go` took a general protection fault
         at its first instruction (08:00000000000206eb). include/arch/amd64/
         elf.h had been copied from x86 when the port began -- "for now we're a
         32bit architecture" -- so the 64-bit firmware recognised only the one
         ELF class long mode cannot execute, and passed every ELF64 by.
         And `load` of an unrecognised file left state-valid at the PREVIOUS
         load's -1: after hello-x86 (-1), `load` of an ELF64 printed ok and
         state-valid still read -1, so `go` would have re-entered a stale
         image. A record that outlived its subject, bug class #1.
  unix   `load` died inside the read -- "panic: segmentation violation at
         4000000" -- because the hosted door's default load-base is an
         unmapped address (a separate, pre-existing defect the tracks work
         around with $setenv); with a mapped buffer the same copy to 0x20000
         would have hit a host page with nothing there, since start_elf() on
         this target is a stub and nothing can ever be entered.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES, in the order the loader meets it:

  1. init_program() zeroes state-valid FIRST, the way every C load path
     (elf_load, forth_load, fcode_load) already did on its own path, so the
     record is about the image at load-base and nothing else. When no loader
     claims the image it now says so -- "init-program: no loader recognised
     the image at load-base (first bytes ..)" -- and, if the bytes carry the
     ELF magic, elf_explain_not_ours() names the FIELD that is not this
     firmware's: e_ident[CLASS]/[DATA]/[VERSION], and when the class agrees
     e_type, e_machine, e_version, e_phentsize. A kernel for the wrong CPU is
     the thing a person actually boots by mistake; it used to fall through
     eight loaders in silence and surface as "No valid state" at `go`.

  2. elf_init_program() runs elf_gate() in front of the copy. One line per
     refusal -- "elf-gate: REFUSED -- <clause> (both values)" -- and it stops
     at the first clause violated, so a one-clause fixture names one clause:
       - e_phnum over MAX_HEADERS; the phdr table past the loaded bytes
       - PT_PHDR / PT_INTERP more than once, or after a PT_LOAD -- the gABI's
         ordering sentence, in the SAME words dsl/elf.fth's ?ph-order prints,
         on purpose: the Forth reader is this gate's agreement oracle on the
         little-endian doors
       - a PT_LOAD whose p_offset + p_filesz runs past load-size
       - p_filesz > p_memsz
       - p_vaddr + p_memsz wrapping the address space
       - the destination overlapping the firmware image [_start,_end), the
         dictionary [dict, dict+dictlimit) or the load buffer it is copied
         from -- compared in the address space the copy uses (virtual, which
         on x86 is the relocated one)
       - no PT_LOAD at all; e_entry inside no PT_LOAD
       - and LAST, after every structural clause has passed: a target that
         cannot place segments at all (elf_segments_placeable, cleared by
         arch/unix's arch_init) -- so the hosted door still exercises every
         clause and its top rung is LOADED, the image at load-base, unrun.
     The bytes stay at load-base for inspection; state-valid stays 0; `go`
     says "No valid state" instead of running.

  3. include/arch/amd64/elf.h says ELFCLASS64 / ELFDATA2LSB / EM_X86_64 with
     Elf64 types, and FMT_elf in include/arch/amd64/types.h widens to match
     (the build refused the old "%#x" against an Elf64_Addr, correctly).

ONE THING THE FIRST DRAFT GOT WRONG, caught by the fixture aimed at ppc's
entry point: arch/ppc/qemu/ldscript places _end after the reset vector at
0xfffffffc and ALIGN(4096)s it, so the symbol wraps to 0 and [_start,_end) was
empty -- the payload that overlapped the whole ROM was refused only by the
dictionary clause, and an 8-byte segment on the entry page was accepted and
copied. A wrapped end means the firmware occupies the top of the address
space, and the gate now says so.

Measured after, on all four doors (smoke-openbios.sh elf-ladder): every
one-clause fixture refused by name before any byte moves; each door's own
firmware ELF refused as the overlap it is, prompt intact; the other class's
good image refused by field; state-valid 0 after every refusal and after a
non-image; good.elf, authored in each door's own class, LOADED (state-valid
-1) and then RUN AND RETURNED -- x86 and amd64 write 'R' to COM1 and ret onto
the return address the firmware planted, ppc blr's, and the prompt comes back;
the sibling lab's hello client still runs and exits on x86 and ppc.

Not covered, and said so: ppc's fw_cfg kernel path (check_preloaded_kernel)
calls arch_init_program() directly and never meets init-program; the C `load`
in libopenbios/load.c (elf_load(), which has its own check_mem_ranges) is not
on any of this lab's doors. Both are untouched.

--- a/libopenbios/elf_load.c
+++ b/libopenbios/elf_load.c
@@ -469,6 +469,185 @@
     return retval;
 }
 
+/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
+ * LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab, patch 68): THE ELF GATE.
+ *
+ * Upstream's elf_init_program() copies every PT_LOAD to its p_vaddr and only
+ * then sets up the program state, so the irreversible step of a `load` is the
+ * memcpy below -- not `go`. Nothing stood in front of it: no bounds check on
+ * the segment against the file, no check of the destination against the
+ * firmware that is doing the copying. Measured 2026-09-05, before this gate,
+ * on all four of this lab's doors:
+ *
+ *   x86    `load` of the firmware's own coreboot-payload ELF (PT_LOAD at
+ *          0x100000, where the firmware's code lives in its own address
+ *          space) never returned -- the copy went over the running firmware
+ *   ppc    `load` of openbios-qemu.elf (PT_LOAD at 0xfff00000, the ROM the
+ *          firmware runs from) printed "Ignoring failed claim" and hung
+ *   amd64  `load` of a 32-bit i386 client was ACCEPTED (state-valid -1) and
+ *          `go` took a general protection fault at its first instruction;
+ *          `load` of an unrecognised file left state-valid at -1 from the
+ *          load before it, so `go` would have re-entered a stale image
+ *   unix   `load` of that client copied to 0x20000 in a host process with
+ *          nothing mapped there: "panic: segmentation violation"
+ *
+ * The gate runs BEFORE the copy, refuses BY NAME (one line, the clause and
+ * both values), leaves the bytes at load-base for inspection, and leaves
+ * state-valid 0 so `go` says so. The gABI's ordering rule is the same sentence
+ * dsl/elf.fth's ?ph-order prints, on purpose: the Forth reader is this gate's
+ * agreement oracle on the little-endian doors.
+ * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
+
+extern unsigned char *dict;
+extern cell dictlimit;
+
+int elf_segments_placeable = 1;
+
+#define ELF_GATE_MAX_PHNUM	0x20	/* elf_load()'s MAX_HEADERS, same limit */
+
+static int overlaps(unsigned long a0, unsigned long a1,
+                    unsigned long b0, unsigned long b1)
+{
+    /* half-open [a0,a1) against [b0,b1) */
+    return a0 < b1 && b0 < a1;
+}
+
+#define REFUSE(fmt, args...) do { \
+        printk("elf-gate: REFUSED -- " fmt "\n", ##args); return 0; } while (0)
+
+/* Returns 1 when every PT_LOAD may be copied; 0 after printing why not. */
+static int elf_gate(const Elf_ehdr *ehdr, unsigned long base,
+                    unsigned long load_size)
+{
+    const Elf_phdr *phdr;
+    unsigned long tabsz, fw0, fw1, d0, d1, lb0, lb1, s0, s1;
+    unsigned long entry = ehdr->e_entry;
+    int i, n = ehdr->e_phnum, seen_load = 0, n_phdr = 0, n_interp = 0;
+    int entry_in_load = 0;
+
+    if (n > ELF_GATE_MAX_PHNUM)
+        REFUSE("too many program headers (e_phnum=%d, limit %d)",
+               n, ELF_GATE_MAX_PHNUM);
+    tabsz = (unsigned long)n * sizeof(Elf_phdr);
+    if (ehdr->e_phoff > load_size || tabsz > load_size - ehdr->e_phoff)
+        REFUSE("the program-header table runs past the loaded bytes "
+               "(e_phoff=%lx + %d*%d > load-size=%lx)",
+               (unsigned long)ehdr->e_phoff, n, (int)sizeof(Elf_phdr),
+               load_size);
+    phdr = (const Elf_phdr *)(base + ehdr->e_phoff);
+
+    fw0 = (unsigned long)&_start;  fw1 = (unsigned long)&_end;
+    /* arch/ppc/qemu/ldscript puts _end AFTER the reset vector at 0xfffffffc
+     * and ALIGN(4096)s it, so the symbol wraps to 0 and [_start,_end) would be
+     * empty -- the payload that overlapped the whole ROM was caught only by
+     * the dictionary clause below, measured 2026-09-05. A firmware whose end
+     * wrapped occupies the top of the address space: say so. */
+    if (fw1 <= fw0)
+        fw1 = (unsigned long)-1;
+    d0  = (unsigned long)dict;     d1  = d0 + (unsigned long)dictlimit;
+    lb0 = base;                    lb1 = base + load_size;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+        switch (phdr[i].p_type) {
+        case PT_PHDR:
+            if (++n_phdr > 1)
+                REFUSE("more than one PT_PHDR (gABI: at most once)");
+            if (seen_load)
+                REFUSE("PT_PHDR after a PT_LOAD (gABI: it must precede "
+                       "every loadable segment)");
+            continue;
+        case PT_INTERP:
+            if (++n_interp > 1)
+                REFUSE("more than one PT_INTERP (gABI: at most once)");
+            if (seen_load)
+                REFUSE("PT_INTERP after a PT_LOAD (gABI: it must precede "
+                       "every loadable segment)");
+            continue;
+        case PT_LOAD:
+            break;
+        default:
+            continue;
+        }
+        seen_load = 1;
+        if (phdr[i].p_offset > load_size
+            || phdr[i].p_filesz > load_size - phdr[i].p_offset)
+            REFUSE("PT_LOAD %d runs past the end of the file "
+                   "(p_offset=%lx + p_filesz=%lx > load-size=%lx)", i,
+                   (unsigned long)phdr[i].p_offset,
+                   (unsigned long)phdr[i].p_filesz, load_size);
+        if (phdr[i].p_filesz > phdr[i].p_memsz)
+            REFUSE("PT_LOAD %d has p_filesz > p_memsz (%lx > %lx; gABI: "
+                   "p_memsz is at least p_filesz)", i,
+                   (unsigned long)phdr[i].p_filesz,
+                   (unsigned long)phdr[i].p_memsz);
+        s0 = phdr[i].p_vaddr;
+        if (phdr[i].p_memsz > (unsigned long)-1 - s0)
+            REFUSE("PT_LOAD %d wraps the address space (p_vaddr=%lx "
+                   "p_memsz=%lx)", i, s0, (unsigned long)phdr[i].p_memsz);
+        s1 = s0 + phdr[i].p_memsz;
+        if (!phdr[i].p_memsz)
+            continue;
+        if (overlaps(s0, s1, fw0, fw1))
+            REFUSE("PT_LOAD %d [%lx..%lx) overlaps the firmware image "
+                   "[%lx..%lx) -- the copy would go over the code doing it",
+                   i, s0, s1, fw0, fw1);
+        if (dictlimit && overlaps(s0, s1, d0, d1))
+            REFUSE("PT_LOAD %d [%lx..%lx) overlaps the dictionary "
+                   "[%lx..%lx)", i, s0, s1, d0, d1);
+        if (overlaps(s0, s1, lb0, lb1))
+            REFUSE("PT_LOAD %d [%lx..%lx) overlaps the load buffer "
+                   "[%lx..%lx) it would be copied from", i, s0, s1, lb0, lb1);
+        if (entry >= s0 && entry < s1)
+            entry_in_load = 1;
+    }
+    if (!seen_load)
+        REFUSE("no PT_LOAD at all -- nothing to enter");
+    if (!entry_in_load)
+        REFUSE("e_entry=%lx lies inside no PT_LOAD", entry);
+    if (!elf_segments_placeable)
+        REFUSE("this hosted firmware cannot place segments (its start_elf "
+               "is a stub) -- the image stays at load-base, LOADED and "
+               "unrun");
+    return 1;
+}
+
+#undef REFUSE
+
+/* The bytes carry the ELF magic but is_elf() said no. Say which field, so a
+ * wrong-arch kernel is refused by name instead of falling silently through
+ * every loader. Returns 1 if it printed (it was an ELF), 0 if not an ELF. */
+int
+elf_explain_not_ours(const unsigned char *b)
+{
+    const Elf_ehdr *e = (const Elf_ehdr *)b;
+
+    if (b[EI_MAG0] != ELFMAG0 || b[EI_MAG1] != ELFMAG1
+        || b[EI_MAG2] != ELFMAG2 || b[EI_MAG3] != ELFMAG3)
+        return 0;
+    printk("elf-gate: REFUSED -- an ELF, but not this firmware's:");
+    if (b[EI_CLASS] != ARCH_ELF_CLASS)
+        printk(" e_ident[CLASS]=%d (want %d)", b[EI_CLASS], ARCH_ELF_CLASS);
+    if (b[EI_DATA] != ARCH_ELF_DATA)
+        printk(" e_ident[DATA]=%d (want %d)", b[EI_DATA], ARCH_ELF_DATA);
+    if (b[EI_VERSION] != EV_CURRENT)
+        printk(" e_ident[VERSION]=%d (want %d)", b[EI_VERSION], EV_CURRENT);
+    /* The fields after e_ident are read at the WRONG class's offsets when the
+     * class differs, so name them only when the class agrees. */
+    if (b[EI_CLASS] == ARCH_ELF_CLASS && b[EI_DATA] == ARCH_ELF_DATA) {
+        if (e->e_type != ET_EXEC)
+            printk(" e_type=%d (want ET_EXEC=%d)", (int)e->e_type, ET_EXEC);
+        if (!ARCH_ELF_MACHINE_OK(e->e_machine))
+            printk(" e_machine=%d (not this CPU)", (int)e->e_machine);
+        if (e->e_version != EV_CURRENT)
+            printk(" e_version=%d (want %d)", (int)e->e_version, EV_CURRENT);
+        if (e->e_phentsize != sizeof(Elf_phdr))
+            printk(" e_phentsize=%d (want %d)", (int)e->e_phentsize,
+                   (int)sizeof(Elf_phdr));
+    }
+    printk("\n");
+    return 1;
+}
+
 void 
 elf_init_program(void)
 {
